(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "adr-2609010100-a-required-check-that-cannot-pass-is-bypass-training")
  (:adr/title "A required check that can never pass is the dual of a gate that can never fail, and worse")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-01")
  (:adr/body "# Context

`kotoba-lang/kotoba` shipped v0.7.3 with a WebAssembly emitter that writes a
`call` operand as one byte, so any module needing function index 128 or above
loads nowhere. The compiler exits 0 while doing it. The fix already existed
upstream; the release shipped a pin three commits short of it.

Asking how that reached users produced a more general answer than a stale pin.

# What was measured

`main`'s head had **zero check runs**, and the last successful CI run on `main`
was 2026-08-29T02:37 -- before v0.7.3 was published at 09:56 the same day.
Eleven commits landed between the tag and this investigation with no CI signal
on `main` at any point. One of them, a `clojure.data.json` retirement, left the
tree red in four places: two `ArityException`s and two assertion failures that
were the same exception swallowed by a `try`.

The reason is a branch protection rule that cannot be satisfied:

```
required_status_checks.contexts = [\"CI\"]
required_pull_request_reviews   = enabled
```

Nothing produces a context named `CI`. The workflow is named `CI`, but its
check-runs are named per job. Measured on `main`:

- check-run names: `[\"CLJ launcher gates\", \"Python SDK gates\"]`
- legacy commit statuses on the last five commits: `0, 0, 0, 0, 0`

So no pull request can satisfy that context, green or not. Merges land through
the owner account instead.

# Decision

Treat \"a required check that can never pass\" as the same class of defect as
\"a check that can never fail\", and name it in the same place.

This workspace already holds that **a gate which never goes red is theatre**
(CLAUDE.md, fleet-ci). The dual is worse, because it is not merely uninformative:

- A gate that never fails is ignored.
- **A gate that can never pass is bypassed**, and a bypass does not read the
  result of the checks that did run.

The protection rule did not fail to catch the `data.json` regression. It was
never able to. Every merge is a bypass, and a bypass is indifferent to whether
CI was green, red, or absent.

# Consequences

- Point `required_status_checks.contexts` at names that are actually produced
  (`CLJ launcher gates`, `Python SDK gates`), or add one summary job named `CI`
  that the others gate. Until then the rule is a formality.
- `ci.yml` has `pull_request` and `push: main` triggers and no
  `workflow_dispatch`, so there is no way to run CI against `main`'s own head
  without opening a pull request. Getting the measurement in this ADR required
  opening one for that purpose.
- The release that closes kotoba#526 stays held until `main` is green by a
  check someone can actually read.

# Boundaries

This is about one repository's settings, measured on 2026-09-01. It is not a
claim about the other repositories in this workspace; none of them were checked.
The general shape -- a required context naming a workflow rather than its jobs
-- is worth looking for elsewhere, but looking for it is not the same as having
looked.

# Verification

Every number here came from the GitHub API rather than from reading the
workflow: `branches/main/protection`, `commits/{sha}/check-runs`,
`commits/{sha}/status`, and `gh run list`. The `data.json` failure counts came
from CI logs on kotoba#527, and the fix was verified in both directions -- the
added tests raise six errors against the previous implementation and pass
against the new one.
")
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2608312100-build-scaling-benchmark-and-what-it-refuses-to-claim"))))
